UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 3, Lesson 1: Sources of evidence: photographs and objects', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A primary source was made at the time being studied (photo, object, letter, map). A secondary source was made later (textbook, documentary). To use a source, ask what it is, who made it, when, what it shows and what it does not show.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA museum holds a heavy iron kettle with a curved handle. What can you infer, and what can you not know from the kettle alone?\n\nFEEDBACK\nIt was probably used to heat water over a fire or stove, and the material is iron. You cannot know who owned it or the exact year without a label or another source.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving that a photo shows everything truthfully ignores that someone chose what to photograph and what to leave out.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nChoose one photo or object from a family, library or museum collection. Complete a source record: type, primary or secondary, clues, one inference and one remaining question. Compare it with a second source.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"A primary source was made at the time being studied (photo, object, letter, map). A secondary source was made later (textbook, documentary). To use a source, ask what it is, who made it, when, what it shows and what it does not show.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-HIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Choose one photo or object from a family, library or museum collection. Complete a source record: type, primary or secondary, clues, one inference and one remaining question. Compare it with a second source. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using source on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Believing that a photo shows everything truthfully ignores that someone chose what to photograph and what to leave out.. Correction: It was probably used to heat water over a fire or stove, and the material is iron.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing that a photo shows everything truthfully ignores that someone chose what to photograph and what to leave out. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating source on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with source, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about source with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for source. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for source. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing that a photo shows everything truthfully ignores that someone chose what to photograph and what to leave out","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","It was probably used to heat water over a fire or stove, and the material is iron"],"question":"A museum holds a heavy iron kettle with a curved handle. What can you infer, and what can you not know from the kettle alone?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for source; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["It was probably used to heat water over a fire or stove, and the material is iron","Careful use of primary and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Believing that a photo shows everything truthfully ignores that someone chose what to photograph and what to leave out","A primary source was made at the time being studied (photo, object, letter, map)"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with source?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named primary and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Believing that a photo shows everything truthfully ignores that someone chose what to photograph and what to leave out","Name primary and explain why it matters only, without linking it to source","Apply source to new material and name the primary and explain why it matters used"],"question":"Which response best applies source with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses sources, places or historical/geographic evidence appropriately for this lesson on source. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing that a photo shows everything truthfully ignores that someone chose what to photograph and what to leave out","It was probably used to heat water over a fire or stove, and the material is iron","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or place detail","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail"],"question":"Which response best uses sources, places or historical/geographic evidence for source?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing that a photo shows everything truthfully ignores that someone chose what to photograph and what to leave out","Name a specific source or place detail and say what it shows about source","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or place detail","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about source?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (A primary source was made at the time being studied (photo, object) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Example photo: shopfront sign \"Mills Bakery\", a horse and cart and people in long coats","Believing that a photo shows everything truthfully ignores that someone chose what to photograph and what to leave out","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or place detail","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about source?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Check facts, acknowledge limits of the evidence, and avoid inventing details","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue"],"question":"Which action keeps sources, places or historical/geographic evidence accurate for source?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Choose one photo or object from a family, library or museum collection","Retype the worked example with no new example","Believing that a photo shows everything truthfully ignores that someone chose what to photograph and what to leave out","Name primary and explain why it matters only, with no linked example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies source to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about source with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating source on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using source on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for source. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with source, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Believing that a photo shows everything truthfully ignores that someone chose what to photograph and what to leave out.. Correction: It was probably used to heat water over a fire or stove, and the material is iron.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y3-w03-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise source.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"History","success_criteria":["I can use source with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify primary and explain why it matters.","I can practise secondary independently on a new prompt.","I can notice inference needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"A primary source was made at the time being studied (photo, object, letter, map).","options":["A claim unrelated to the source or place","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","A primary source was made at the time being studied (photo, object, letter, map)"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about source with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample photo: shopfront sign \"Mills Bakery\", a horse and cart and people in long coats. It shows a bakery and transport of the time. It does not show who owned the shop or what the bread cost, so those questions need other sources.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA museum holds a heavy iron kettle with a curved handle. What can you infer, and what can you not know from the kettle alone?\n\nFEEDBACK\nIt was probably used to heat water over a fire or stove, and the material is iron. You cannot know who owned it or the exact year without a label or another source.","explain":"A primary source was made at the time being studied (photo, object, letter, map). A secondary source was made later (textbook, documentary). To use a source, ask what it is, who made it, when, what it shows and what it does not show.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing that a photo shows everything truthfully ignores that someone chose what to photograph and what to leave out. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Primary and secondary sources.","caption":"Date and maker matter.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Primary</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">made at the time</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Secondary</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">made later</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example photo: shopfront sign \"Mills Bakery\", a horse and cart and people in long coats. It shows a bakery and transport Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Example photo: shopfront sign \"Mills Bakery\", a horse and cart and people in long coats. It shows a bakery and transport Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","options":["A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the source or place","Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about source with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample photo: shopfront sign \"Mills Bakery\", a horse and cart and people in long coats. It shows a bakery and transport of the time. It does not show who owned the shop or what the bread cost, so those questions need other sources. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"What a photo can and cannot show.","caption":"A single source leaves questions.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Photo shows</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">bakery sign, horse cart</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Photo hides</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">owner, prices</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison","A claim unrelated to the source or place","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about source with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving that a photo shows everything truthfully ignores that someone chose what to photograph and what to leave out. Here is a more productive model: Example photo: shopfront sign \"Mills Bakery\", a horse and cart and people in long coats. It shows a bakery and transport of the time. It does not show who owned the shop or what the bread cost, so those questions need other sources.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nIt was probably used to heat water over a fire or stove, and the material is iron. You cannot know who owned it or the exact year without a label or another source.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Believing that a photo shows everything truthfully ignores that someone chose what to photograph and what to leave out. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the source or place","Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about source with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA primary source was made at the time being studied (photo, object, letter, map). A secondary source was made later (textbook, documentary). To use a source, ask what it is, who made it, when, what it shows and what it does not show. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Transfer to a new inquiry"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 3, Lesson 1: Sources of evidence: photographs and objects","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Our local community and its history","week":3,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample photo: shopfront sign \"Mills Bakery\", a horse and cart and people in long coats. It shows a bakery and transport of the time. It does not show who owned the shop or what the bread cost, so those questions need other sources.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the sources, places or community details used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Primary and secondary sources.","caption":"Date and maker matter.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Primary</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">made at the time</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Secondary</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">made later</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"What a photo can and cannot show.","caption":"A single source leaves questions.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Photo shows</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">bakery sign, horse cart</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Photo hides</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">owner, prices</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-hsie-y3-w03-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
